1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O b5 6. Bb3 Be7 7. d4 d6 8. c3
O-O 9. h3 exd4 10. cxd4 Na5 11. Bc2 c5 {A terrible move. I had hardly
considered the dangers of allowing White's e5.} 12. dxc5 dxc5 13. Qe2 Qc7 ({
Another bad move. Perhaps} 13... Bb7 {instead.}) 14. e5 Nh5 ({The position is
quite lost so I thought that after g4 and gxh5 I might have some slight
attacking chances on the kingside. If} 14... Nd5 15. Qe4 {wins the knight and
I have no chances.}) ({But} 14... Nd7 {might be playable since since after} 15.
Qe4 g6 16. Qxa8 Bb7 17. Qxf8+ Bxf8 {White has an advantage but Black's
position is reasonable.}) 15. g4 Nc6 16. gxh5 Bxh3 17. Re1 f5 18. Bf4 Bg4 19.
Qe3 ({This move gives me chances. Perhaps} 19. Nbd2 {is better.}) 19... Nb4 {
I feel as though I'm back in the game.} 20. Bd1 Rad8 21. a3 Rd3 {I think this
is the only move that maintains some pressure.} 22. axb4 (22. Qc1 {is answered
by} Nd5 {which regains my piece.}) 22... Rxe3 23. Rxe3 cxb4 24. Nbd2 Rd8 25.
Bb3+ Kh8 26. Rxa6 Bxh5 {This pawn had now to be captured as 27 h6 is a threat.
White's position is not as easy as it looks. Black is threatening ...g5-g4.}
27. Nf1 $4 {An outright blunder. I am most certainly back in the game now.}
Bxf3 28. Rd6 Bxd6 29. exd6 Qc6 30. Re7 Bd5 31. Rc7 Qa8 32. Bc2 Be6 33. Ne3 Qa1+
34. Kg2 Qxb2 35. Re7 Qf6 36. Nd1 Qg6+ 37. Kf1 Bc4+ 38. Ke1 Re8 39. Bxf5 Rxe7+
40. dxe7 Qe8 41. Bd6 b3 42. Kd2 Bf7 43. Nc3 Qc6 44. Ba3 Qc4 45. Ne2 b4 46. Bb2
Qc7 47. Nd4 Qxe7 48. Bd3 Qg5+ 49. Ke1 Bh5 50. Nxb3 Qd5 (50... Qg1+ {would be
better.}) 51. Nd4 Qa2 52. Bc1 b3 53. Bc4 b2 {Source: Jimmy Adams via Gerald
Hartmann. Notes by Griff Parsonage.} 0-1

